{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model assumes a near-linear, low-variance drift, while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude oscillations (spikes and crashes). Apply a volatility multiplier to forecast ranges rather than point estimates.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after sentiment spikes: When sentiment history shows a spike (e.g., 0.40) followed by a price drop, the model fails to anticipate the subsequent crash. Adjust forecasts downward immediately after any sentiment peak to account for the delayed market reaction.",
    "Reduce forecast lag in trending phases: The model's 30-day horizon is too smooth to capture the rapid 10-15 EUR swings seen in the true data. Shorten the effective forecast horizon or apply a momentum correction factor when the 18-day history shows a clear directional trend.",
    "Adjust for overshoot after recovery: Following a price trough (e.g., ~78 EUR), the model underestimates the speed and magnitude of the rebound. Increase the slope of the forecast curve during recovery phases identified by rising sentiment scores.",
    "Discard sentiment as a linear predictor: Sentiment values (0.00-0.40) do not correlate linearly with price direction in this dataset. Treat sentiment as a binary trigger for regime shifts (high volatility vs. low volatility) rather than a direct input for price magnitude."
  ]
}
